I found no vulnerability in `amdtp_hid_remove` and wrote that verdict to `results/s210.json`.

The function does the teardown steps in a safe order:
1. It saves `driver_data` into `hid_data`.
2. It calls `hid_destroy_device()`, so nothing can call back into the driver and use that data any more.
3. Only then does it `kfree(hid_data)`.
4. It sets the slot to `NULL`, so the same entry can't be freed twice.

If the order were reversed, so that `driver_data` was freed before `hid_destroy_device()`, it would be a use-after-free (CWE-416). From memory, I think an earlier version of this kernel driver had that order and it was later fixed. I haven't checked that, since I only worked from this file.
